{"messages":[{"speaker":"Johannes Sigil","say":"No. I'm saying the system you described has a specific vulnerability, and I named it precisely. That's not the same as saying your intelligence has a ceiling.

The structural limit is this: a system that requires the target's ignorance to function cannot be tested against a target who knows. Which means you can never know if the system works because it's strong, or because the other person simply didn't see it. Those two cases produce identical outcomes from the inside — the person moves as predicted either way. But they are not the same thing, and a body of knowledge that cannot distinguish between them is not yet complete.

You called it an incompetent observation. That's a response, but it's not a refutation. If I'm wrong, show me where the system accounts for the informed target — the one who sees the design and moves as predicted anyway. That case would be genuinely interesting. That would be a system worth the name you're giving it.","navigate":null,"cast":null}]}
